(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-quiz"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is scaling described?","choices":["Thickening and hardening of the skin","Accumulation of loose or adherent cornified fragments of the epidermis, typically grey or white","A dried crust of serum","A scratch or abrasion in which the top layer of the skin is worn away by repeated rubbing or friction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which test should be considered before starting substantial compression for stasis dermatitis?","choices":["A punch biopsy of the affected gaiter skin","A bacterial culture taken from the affected skin","An ankle-brachial index or toe pressure","A venous duplex ultrasound of the affected leg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — arterial status has to be clarified first, because compression on an ischaemic limb causes harm. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What is lichenification?","choices":["Accumulation of loose cornified fragments of the epidermis","A break in the skin","Abnormal formation of connective tissue","Thickening and hardening of the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it develops from chronic rubbing or scratching. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which education points are named for atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Apply emollient after rinsing, avoid irritants, use fragrance-free skincare, demonstrate quantity and application, and give a written flare and infection action plan","Bathe as little as possible, avoid all moisturisers, use only prescription cleansers, and stop any topical steroid at the first sign of improvement","Apply the strongest available steroid at the first sign of a flare, then stop abruptly","Avoid all sun exposure and apply broad-spectrum sunscreen several times daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — corticosteroid concerns are addressed directly rather than avoided. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the foundation of treatment for diaper dermatitis?","choices":["A high-potency topical corticosteroid until resolution","Reducing moisture and irritant exposure through frequent changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and superabsorbent diapers, with a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at each change","A topical antifungal applied at every diaper change regardless of cause, together with a course of oral antibiotics to prevent secondary bacterial infection of the damaged barrier","Oral antibiotics to prevent secondary infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The previous barrier layer need not be scrubbed away if it remains clean. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which topical corticosteroid is classified as mild?","choices":["Clobetasol propionate 0.05%","Hydrocortisone, at all strengths","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%","Betamethasone valerate 0.1%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — 0.1%, 0.5%, 1% and 2.5% all sit in the mild tier. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the herald patch, and how often does it occur?","choices":["A coin-shaped plaque occurring in half of cases","A single 2 to 5 cm round or oval, sharply demarcated pink or salmon plaque on the chest, neck or back, occurring in 50 to 90% of cases","A raindrop-shaped papule occurring in every case","A single tense bulla on the trunk, occurring in about a third of cases before the wider eruption appears elsewhere"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it precedes the general eruption by one to two weeks. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"How does first-line treatment of alopecia areata differ between children and adults?","choices":["Oral minoxidil first line in children; oral finasteride first line in adults","Phototherapy first line in children; systemic immunosuppression first line in adults","Topical steroids first line in children ten and under; intralesional steroids first line in adolescents and adults","Intralesional steroids first line in children ten and under; topical steroids first line in adults"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — injections are avoided in young children. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A fluid-filled lesion greater than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A nodule","A bulla","A vesicle","A pustule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a vesicle is the same lesion up to one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which scale classifies skin type by its response to ultraviolet light?","choices":["The Fitzpatrick scale","The SCORTEN score","The Auspitz sign","The Nikolsky sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — six types, from always burns and never tans through to never burns. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which medications can produce lichenoid reactions?","choices":["Statins and proton pump inhibitors","Topical corticosteroids","Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, sulfonamides, tetracyclines, hydrochlorothiazide, quinidine and some beta blockers","Penicillins, cephalosporins and the macrolide antibiotics, taken either together as a class or individually"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — worth asking about, since the eruption can mimic idiopathic lichen planus. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 173"},{"q":"How is a poorly demarcated lesion described?","choices":["Irregular or blotchy in appearance, without well-defined borders","Smaller than one centimetre in every dimension measured","Present in more than one body region","Raised above the surrounding skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — as against a well demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does treatment of allergic contact dermatitis change when the eruption is extensive rather than limited?","choices":["A more potent topical corticosteroid is applied to a larger surface area instead","An oral antibiotic is added, since extensive disease is usually secondarily infected","Phototherapy is started three times weekly over a five-week course","High-dose oral corticosteroids replace the high-potency topical steroid used for limited disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — soothing measures such as oatmeal baths and cool compresses are used throughout. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for alopecia areata by age group?","choices":["Oral finasteride in adults and topical minoxidil in children under the age of ten years","Systemic corticosteroids in all age groups","Intralesional steroids in adolescents and adults; topical steroids in children aged ten and under","Topical minoxidil at every age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and psychological support is named as one of the most important considerations. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"Which androgen is chiefly responsible for male-pattern hair loss?","choices":["Dihydrotestosterone","Testosterone acting directly on the follicle","Oestradiol","Cortisol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — male-pattern hair loss is an androgen-dependent trait with a strong paternal influence on risk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"Which hand-care advice is given for dyshidrotic eczema?","choices":["Wash the hands as infrequently as possible and avoid all emollients, since they trap the vesicles","Wear occlusive gloves continuously, including overnight and during sleep","Lukewarm water and soap-free cleansers, dry thoroughly, then emollient immediately and as often as possible","Hot water with an antibacterial soap, air dry, then a light lotion once daily"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside avoiding detergents, solvents, hair lotions or dyes, and acidic foods. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Steadily progressive, with universal involvement by adulthood","Curable with a single course of a potent topical corticosteroid","Chronic and relapsing, but many patients improve with age","Self-limiting over six to eight weeks with no recurrence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it may improve, persist or recur. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How is mild perioral dermatitis treated once the corticosteroid is stopped?","choices":["A higher-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily","Topical ketoconazole","Oral prednisone","Topical metronidazole, erythromycin, pimecrolimus or azelaic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with oral tetracycline or doxycycline for more extensive or persistent disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What is a Tzanck smear used for, and what is preferred for confirmation?","choices":["Rapid detection of fungal elements in skin, hair or nail, with fungal culture preferred for confirmation of the species","Detection of the scabies mite, with dermoscopy preferred for confirmation","Detection of immune deposits, with serum ELISA preferred for confirmation","Rapid evaluation of vesicular lesions for herpesvirus cytologic changes, with polymerase chain reaction preferred for confirmation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the smear is fast, the polymerase chain reaction is definitive. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does light microscopy show in bullous pemphigoid?","choices":["Granular immunoglobulin A deposited at the tips of the dermal papillae","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Acantholytic keratinocytes separated from one another within the epidermis","A band-like infiltrate of lymphocytes filling the papillary dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a distinctive pattern at the level where the split occurs. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for seborrhoeic dermatitis?","choices":["Topical antifungals such as ketoconazole, with steroids used early to reduce inflammation","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone, continued long term as monotherapy","Oral terbinafine","Oral doxycycline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Scalp disease uses ketoconazole or selenium sulfide shampoo; facial disease uses ketoconazole cream or lotion. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How does the timing of irritant contact dermatitis vary with the irritant?","choices":["Mild irritants such as soaps may progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present within minutes or be delayed up to 24 hours or more","All irritants produce symptoms within minutes of exposure","All irritants produce symptoms 48 to 72 hours after exposure regardless of whether the agent is a mild soap or a concentrated acid","Timing does not vary with the irritant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the agent determines the tempo. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What characterises pustular psoriasis of the Von Zumbusch type?","choices":["A slowly progressive eruption of silvery scaling plaques developing over many months on the extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees, without fever, chills, malaise or any systemic upset at all","Raindrop papules following streptococcal infection","Joint pain with sausage digits and nail pitting","Abrupt, life-threatening onset with several days of fever then a generalised eruption of 2 to 3 mm pustules over trunk and extremities including nail beds, palms and soles, which may form lakes of pus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it may follow systemic steroid withdrawal. It is commonly mistaken for bacterial or viral infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"What determines whether lichen simplex chronicus recurs?","choices":["Whether the initiating itch is controlled, since recurrence is common if it is not","Whether the plaque was biopsied before treatment was started","Whether a superpotent rather than a potent steroid was used","Whether the patient was over sixty years of age at the first presentation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — sleep, anxiety, neuropathic symptoms and the primary dermatosis all need addressing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"Which test is ordered in nummular eczema when tinea corporis cannot be ruled out?","choices":["A bacterial culture and sensitivity","Allergy patch testing of a standard series","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin","A potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial culture is added if lesions look secondarily infected. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which dermoscopic findings support alopecia areata?","choices":["Comedone-like openings and a stuck-on surface appearance","Yellow dots, black dots, broken hairs, tapered hairs and short regrowth","Finger-like projections with a moth-eaten border at the edge","A dense dark head with the body, eggs and burrow trailing behind"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and dermoscopy strengthens it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 139"},{"q":"What terms describe complete scalp and complete body hair loss in alopecia areata?","choices":["Androgenetic alopecia for scalp and alopecia totalis for body","Alopecia totalis for all scalp hair, alopecia universalis for all body hair","Alopecia universalis for all scalp hair, alopecia totalis for all body hair","Alopecia areata for scalp and alopecia diffusa for body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 137"},{"q":"Where is minoxidil primarily effective?","choices":["At the frontal hairline","Over the entire scalp equally","Only in women","In the crown region of the scalp"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it prevents further loss and may produce regrowth, working best with finasteride. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain evaluates which suspected condition?","choices":["Onychomycosis","Psoriatic nail disease","Subungual melanoma","Alopecia areata"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How are severe or extensive allergic contact dermatitis eruptions treated?","choices":["A five-day course of oral corticosteroids without taper","Oral antihistamines alone","High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks, because early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound","High-potency topical steroids alone regardless of how extensive the eruption is across the body surface area"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Limited eruptions can be managed with high-potency topical steroids. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"}]};
